{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22886","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1367"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1367"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_639780237#10_1119041667","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1367","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_639780237#10_1119041667\" , \"end_char\": 10000.000000 , \"headings\": \"The Cost of the Individual Mandate Penalty for the Remaining Uninsured The Cost of the Individual Mandate Penalty for the Remaining Uninsured How the Individual Mandate Works Estimates of the Actual Penalties People Will Face Discussion Methods: Endnotes Topics Tags \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Undocumented immigrants are ineligible for Medicaid and Marketplace coverage. Since CPS data do not directly indicate whether an immigrant is lawfully present, </cover>we draw on the methods underlying the 2013 analysis by the State Health Access Data Assistance Center (SHADAC) and the recommendations made by Van Hook et. al. 2,3 This approach uses the Survey of Income and Program Participation (SIPP) to develop a model that predicts immigration status; it then applies the model to CPS, controlling to state-level estimates of total undocumented population from Department of Homeland Security. For more detail on the immigration imputation used in this analysis, see the technical Appendix B available here. Individuals in tax-filing units with access to an affordable offer of Employer-"},"time":17},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":16,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
