{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22886","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"undocumented immigrants\" \"state-funded\" \"health coverage\" \"medicaid\")"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"undocumented immigrants\" \"state-funded\" \"health coverage\" \"medicaid\")"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_558669800#3_1084780190","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1374","rank":1,"snippet":"138 percent of the federal poverty level (FPL) Children with household incomes up to 210 percent of poverty Pregnant women with incomes up to 193 percent of poverty. Children are eligible for CHIP with household incomes up to 312 percent of poverty. Apple Health for Kids is available to children even if they are undocumented immigrants. In that case, only state Medicaid funds are used to provide coverage, and families with income above 200 percent of the poverty level will have to pay higher premiums to cover a child who is undocumented. Pregnant <cover>undocumented immigrants are also eligible for state-funded Apple Health coverage. How does Medicaid </cover>provide financial assistance to Medicare beneficiaries in Washington? Many Medicare beneficiaries receive Medicaid financial assistance that can help them with Medicare premiums, lower prescription drug costs, and pay for expenses not covered by Medicare – including long-term care. Our guide to financial assistance for Medicare enrollees in Washington includes overviews of these programs, including Medicaid long-term care benefits, Extra Help, and eligibility guidelines for assistance. How do I apply for Medicaid in Washington? In Washington State, most Medicaid enrollment is managed through the state-run exchange, Washington Healthplanfinder.\" , \"start_"},"time":66},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":30,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
