{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22886","model_call":51,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"undocumented immigrants\" \"federally funded\" \"nonemergency medicaid\" chip)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"undocumented immigrants\" \"federally funded\" \"nonemergency medicaid\" chip)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#2_1366945063","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1381","rank":1,"snippet":"elect this option? Is a new state law required to elect the CHIPRA option? Why should a state elect this CHIPRA option? \" , \"segment\": \"In 2009, Congress recognized the importance of preventive health care for children and the harm caused by these restrictions, and removed the mandatory federal five-year waiting period for immigrant children and pregnant women in Medicaid and CHIP. States now can receive federal funding to provide nonemergency coverage to these populations without a waiting period. Does this CHIPRA option provide benefits to undocumented immigrants? No. <cover>Undocumented immigrants remain ineligible for federally funded nonemergency Medicaid and CHIP. </cover>The new CHIPRA option did not change eligibility rules for undocumented immigrants. Remember, however, that eligibility rules apply only to individuals seeking benefits, not to the entire household. Thus if a lawfully residing child is eligible for Medicaid or CHIP as a result of this CHIPRA option, the child’s parents may apply for Medicaid or CHIP for their child, regardless of their own immigration status. Noncitizens whose immigration status renders them ineligible for nonemergency Medicaid are still eligible for emergency Medicaid. Thus, low-income, lawfully residing children and pregnant women may be eligible only ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#2_1366945063","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":71},"model_calls":51,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":26,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-22886","model_call":51,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381"},"model_calls":51,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":26,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22886","model_call":51,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#1_1366942671","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1381","rank":2,"snippet":"that required most lawfully residing immigrants to wait five years before they could be eligible for federally funded Medicaid. This same restriction was applied to CHIP after the program was created in 1997. Nevertheless, many states continued to provide Medicaid and CHIP coverage to lawfully residing immigrants with state funds, as an investment in the health of their residents and as a strategy for reducing inefficient and expensive emergency medical costs. In 2009, Congress recognized the importance of preventive health care for children and the harm caused by these restrictions, and removed the mandatory federal five-year waiting period for immigrant children and pregnant women in Medicaid and CHIP. States now can receive federal funding to provide nonemergency coverage to these populations without a waiting period. Does this CHIPRA option provide benefits to undocumented immigrants? No. <cover>Undocumented immigrants remain ineligible for federally funded nonemergency Medicaid and CHIP.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 950.000000 , \"title\": \"Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage - National Immigration Law Center\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nilc.org/issues/health-care/federal-funding-for-states-to-provide-health-coverage/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#2_1366945063","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":51,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":26,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
