{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-22886","model_call":52,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1381"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_759029027#0_1366939842","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1381","rank":3,"snippet":"<cover>undocumented immigrants? Who is covered under the CHIPRA option? Which categories of immigrants are considered “lawfully residing” under the CHIPRA option? What verification is required? Do immigrants with sponsors need to worry about this new CHIPRA option? How does a state elect this option? Is a new state law required to elect the CHIPRA option? Why should a state elect this CHIPRA option? \" , \"segment\": \"Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage - National Immigration Law Center Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage Facts About Federal Funding for States to Provide Health Coverage to Immigrant Children and Pregnant Women AUGUST 2010 Notes and citations available in the PDF version. The Children’s Health Insurance Program Reauthorization Act of 2009 (CHIPRA) provides states with an opportunity to provide affordable health coverage with federal funding to “lawfully residing” immigrant children and pregnant women through the Medicaid and Children’s Health Insurance Program (CHIP). Through this federal option, states can reduce the number of uninsured immigrant as well as citizen children in their state and can help reduce inequities and disparities in our health care system. This fact sheet highlights the key elements of this new </cover>"},"time":10},"model_calls":52,"document_judgments":46,"relevance_3":27,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
